label interlude_intro
[signal arg="interlude:on"]
[signal arg="bg:history_hall"]
INTERLUDE. Lan does not appear in this chapter.
Enriko: You actually did the reading. Nobody does the reading for General History. I'm fairly sure Dave forgets he assigns it.
Veldero: I like knowing things. It's about the Dark Age today. Origins of Gi Corruption. Should be interesting.
Enriko: You read the syllabus. That's deeply unsettling for a man I consider a friend.
Enriko: You also showed up to Adelaide's advanced casting practical last week. You're Physical. You don't take Shir theory.
Veldero: I like watching people who are good at things. Keeps me honest about my own limits.
A perfectly reasonable answer, delivered in a perfectly reasonable tone.
Enriko let it go the way he let go of most things that weren't immediately useful gossip. Filed, not forgotten.
label lecture
Dave: Dark Age. Evil God descends, world goes to pieces, you know the outline. Today we're talking about why.
Dave: Not the god himself, but what he did to the energy already living inside every creature on this continent.
A small translucent shape bloomed above his palm. Pale gold light folded itself into the outline of a running deer, delicate as smoke.
Dave: Gi lives in everything. Then the corruption came. Not a curse in the storybook sense.
Dave: A poison aimed at gi itself, twisting it, souring it, turning something ordinary into something that eats the mind of whatever carries it.
The deer's outline buckled. Something jagged and wrong grew along its spine.
Dave: A creature exposed to corrupted gi doesn't die. It changes.
Dave: It loses whatever reason it had, and the body warps to match what's left.
Dave: Claws where there weren't claws, hunger where there was an ordinary animal eating ordinary grass. That's a monster.
Dave: Not born. Made. Every one of them used to be something else.
Dave: A monster grows by consuming. Every creature it kills, every ounce of gi it tears out and swallows, it keeps.
Dave: Most never gather enough to matter.
Dave: But if one does, if it keeps piling gi on gi past the point any sane creature would stop[pause=0.35][lspeed=0.25!]...[lspeed]
The illusion convulsed and reassembled into something taller, horned, with eyes that glowed with a cold, aware light.
Dave: [pause=0.35][lspeed=0.25!]...[lspeed]it wakes back up. Gets its mind back.
Dave: Gets its cruelty back, and now it remembers being something, and it's furious about what it became to survive.
Dave: That's a Demon. Not a different species. The same corruption, given enough blood to finish the job.
Dave: Write that down. It'll be on the exam. Now. Tax reform.
Beside Enriko, Veldero had gone very still, pen paused over his notes.
label luna
On the way out, Enriko noticed her. Third row from the back, rank sixty-six by the number on her collar.
A girl with a closed-off expression who had sat through the entire lecture without writing a word,
her hands folded so tightly her knuckles had gone pale around the part about the deer.
Enriko: You know her? Sixty-six. She's in your monster hunting group, right?
Veldero: Luna. She doesn't talk much.
Enriko: She looked like she wanted to walk out halfway through. You should say hi. You two are partied up. You're basically obligated by law.
Veldero: I don't think that's a real law.
Enriko: It's an Enriko law. Those are the important ones.
Veldero hesitated a half-second longer than the joke deserved, watching Luna's stiff back disappear through the doorway.
Veldero: Maybe later. She likes her space.
He lingered at the door a moment, looking back at the empty hall where a horned shape made of light had hung in the air.
There were things about this world he understood better than he let anyone see,
and things about the people in it he was still, carefully, working out how to read.
He filed it away, the way he filed most things, and went to catch up with Enriko.
[signal arg="interlude:off"]
[signal arg="chapter_end:ch09"]
[end_timeline]
